The classic interview ask — center a child both ways — is two lines once you know the axis model:

```css
.center-me {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* main axis */
  align-items: center;     /* cross axis */
}
```

That's it. No negative margins, no `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`, no knowing the child's dimensions in advance. It works whether the child is text, an image, or a whole component, and it keeps working if the container resizes.

Say this out loud in an interview and you signal you understand the axis model rather than having copy-pasted a centering snippet: "justify-content handles the main axis, align-items handles the cross axis — together that's both dimensions."

Before flexbox, matching column heights required JS or a CSS hack (`display: table-cell` fought you the whole way). Flex items are equal-height by default along the cross axis, for free:

```css
.columns {
  display: flex; /* items stretch to the tallest sibling automatically */
}
.columns > .col {
  flex: 1;       /* equal width too, if you want it */
}
```

A sticky footer — footer pinned to the bottom of the viewport even on short pages — is the same axis logic, just vertical:

```css
body {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
.main-content {
  flex: 1; /* absorbs all extra vertical space, pushing footer down */
}
```

Both patterns rely on the same idea: give one element `flex: 1` and let it absorb whatever space its siblings don't use.

A flex item with long unbreakable content (a long word, a wide image, a `<pre>` block) often refuses to shrink below its content size, even with `flex-shrink: 1` set and the container clearly too narrow. Text overflows the container instead of wrapping.

The cause: flex items have an **implicit `min-width: auto`** (or `min-height: auto` in a column), which means "never shrink smaller than my content's natural minimum size." `flex-shrink` only kicks in *after* that floor is respected — it never overrides it.

The fix is one line:

```css
.flex-item {
  min-width: 0; /* overrides the auto floor, lets shrink actually shrink it */
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
```

This single gotcha explains most "flexbox is breaking my layout" bug reports: a child not shrinking isn't a `flex-shrink` bug, it's an unset `min-width` bug.

A real navbar combines most of what this topic covered: main-axis spacing, cross-axis centering, and a shrinkable search box.

```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;       /* vertically center logo, links, actions */
  justify-content: space-between; /* logo left, actions right */
  gap: 24px;
  padding: 12px 24px;
}
.navbar .search {
  flex: 1 1 200px; /* grow to fill space, shrink to 200px minimum */
  min-width: 0;    /* let it actually shrink past its content size */
}
.navbar .actions {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 12px;
  flex-shrink: 0;  /* buttons never get squeezed */
}
```

Walking an interviewer through this — "the search box grows to fill available space but has a 200px floor and shrinks correctly because I zeroed its min-width; the action buttons are pinned at `flex-shrink: 0` so they never get crushed" — is exactly the kind of answer that reads as fluent rather than memorized.
